{"reference":"Job 24","book":"Job","chapter":24,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Debate Around Job 24","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 24 belongs to the extended debate that follows Job’s seven days of shared silence with Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar. The friends increasingly interpret Job’s suffering as evidence of wrongdoing, but Job rejects that diagnosis. Read in that setting, this chapter’s attention to people who commit violence, exploit the vulnerable, and yet seem secure is part of Job’s sustained challenge to a simple equation between suffering and guilt.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_024_ca5a5718416e11dea621a417"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"A later interpretation connects Job with endurance and the compassionate outcome of his story. In this reading, Job’s steadfastness means persistence through suffering, not a command to remain silent about wrongs or to forgo protection and escape from abuse. His searching, protest, and refusal of a false explanation remain part of the larger story.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_024_49f3d39b760aa6255e6666c9"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}